  "id": "Compound::DB00728 - Gene::1140",
  "verbalization": "Rocuronium, a neuromuscular blocking agent, is implicated in the pathogenesis of skeletal muscle paralysis through a mechanism involving the binding of its active metabolite, pancuronium, to the Chrnb1 gene. This binding is supported by multiple paths, which collectively suggest that Rocuronium and its metabolite, pancuronium, are both associated with the same biological outcome—skeletal muscle paralysis—through a shared interaction with Chrnb1. The lowest common ancestor of Rocuronium and pancuronium points to the steroid class, indicating a potential common pathway in their action. The evidence also highlights that Rocuronium is included in the category of neuromuscular nondepolarizing blockade, which is a well-documented mechanism of action, further reinforcing its role in causing muscle paralysis. The presence of multiple paths linking Rocuronium to Chrnb1 through different metabolites and pathways suggests a robust, mechanistically consistent relationship, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
